  "facts": [
    "The Wards Island Bridge, originally called the Harlem River Pedestrian Bridge, opened May 18, 1951 after a $2.1 million construction, providing the first permanent footpath between Manhattan and Wards Island.",
    "Between 2010 and 2012, the Wards Island Bridge underwent a $16.8 million overhaul that replaced its deck, steel superstructure, and control systems, restoring 24‑hour, year‑round access for pedestrians and cyclists.",
    "The first link to Wards Island was a wooden drawbridge built in 1807 by Philip Milledolar and Bartholomew Ward, which survived until a storm destroyed it in 1821."
